      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "...if the clause is written to encompass broader systemic failures, which is often the case in modern leases, but you must still argue the impossibility of performance.\n\nRegarding the burden of proof, when the disruption feels like pure chaos—like a sudden, unpredictable surge of bad actors or a massive, systemic failure—courts are generally more sympathetic, but they still demand evidence. They look to see if the chaos was the *direct* trigger that made the inventory impossible to maintain, rather than just making it expensive or difficult. If you can demonstrate that the supply chain collapse was an external, unforeseeable event—not merely a competitor's aggressive pricing strategy—you have a stronger case.\n\nIt's important to differentiate between a \"bad business cycle\" (which is a risk you absorb) and a \"catastrophic system failure\" (which might be force majeure). I strongly recommend having a local Singaporean real estate lawyer review the specific clause against the nature of the disruption.",
      "source": "target",
